{"id":"n-20261024-n726ye","category":"sports","display_title":"Gymnastics Reckoning Ten Years On","display_title_he":"חשבון הנפש בהתעמלות עשר שנים אחרי","short_summary":"A decade after the Larry Nassar abuse scandal shook gymnastics, survivors and advocates gathered to reflect on the slow pace of institutional reform in the sport.","english_level":"C1","words":447,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/24/sexual-assault-scandal-rocked-gymnastics-larry-nassar-sport-change-slow","title":"The sexual assault scandal rocked gymnastics – but has pace of change in the sport been too slow?","published_at":"2026-10-24T05:00:25.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-24T05:00:25.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-24T05:59:21.792Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["A decade has elapsed since the Indianapolis Star published initial allegations of sexual abuse against Larry Nassar, the former team doctor for USA Gymnastics. That pivotal moment triggered an unprecedented global reckoning regarding how athletic organizations protect young competitors. Hundreds of women eventually came forward, exposing not only sexual misconduct but also widespread physical and emotional abuse within the sport across multiple countries, revealing that many nations had critical questions to answer regarding systemic failures.","During Nassar's historic sentencing, Judge Rosemarie Aquilina made the defining decision to permit victims to speak directly in her courtroom. Initially expecting only a dozen names, Aquilina ultimately heard blistering, emotionally charged testimonies from 169 individuals across seven days. She sentenced Nassar to up to 175 years in prison, which combined with other federal and state convictions to total 300 years to life. Survivors noted that speaking directly to their abuser in a secure courtroom environment provided a vital sense of release, allowing them to shed their pain and reclaim their identities. Prominent athletes, including Olympic gold medallists Aly Raisman and Jordyn Wieber, bronze medallist Jamie Dantzscher, and former national champion Mattie Larson, subsequently shared their own experiences, demanding profound accountability from sports administrators. Simone Biles later testified publicly before the US Senate Judiciary Committee.","Two years after the sentencing, the documentary film Athlete A illuminated the traditional coaching structures built on compliance, fear, and strict silence. Kim Shore, a former Canadian gymnast and independent safe sport advocate, watched the survivors on screen and finally understood that her mistreatment was endemic rather than a personal failing. For British gymnast Eloise Jotischky, who won a civil case against British Gymnastics, and Claire Heafford, the documentary provided essential terminology to articulate the coercive, cult-like environments they had endured. Jotischky compared the isolated, closed system of elite gymnastics to a coercively controlling domestic relationship involving a child. Heafford, experiencing profound personal life transitions at the time, was deeply moved by the film and established the alliance Gymnasts for Change to advocate for safer athletic conditions worldwide, eventually bringing survivors together in Rotterdam.","Ten years after the initial revelations, survivors and advocates convened on a houseboat moored in Rotterdam near the World Gymnastics Championships to discuss the ongoing struggle for survivor-led reform. While organizations like USA Gymnastics faced intense scrutiny, cycling through four presidents in under two years, and subsequently adopted new reporting measures, an independent report, an Athlete Bill of Rights, and mandatory safeguarding training, advocates argue that the overall pace of institutional change remains distressingly slow. Institutions such as the US Center for SafeSport have faced heavy criticism, demonstrating that cultivating a genuine culture of safety requires constant, daily vigilance across every single level of the sport."],"facts":[{"fact":"A decade has passed since the Indianapolis Star published allegations against former USA Gymnastics doctor Larry Nassar.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Judge Rosemarie Aquilina presided over Nassar's sentencing hearings, allowing 169 victims to speak over seven days.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Nassar was sentenced to up to 175 years in jail with other convictions, totaling 300 years to life.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"High-profile gymnasts including Simone Biles, Aly Raisman, Jordyn Wieber, and Maggie Nichols spoke out about abuse.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The documentary Athlete A was released two years after Nassar's sentencing, focusing on the systemic culture of compliance and fear.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Survivors and advocates gathered in Rotterdam a decade later to discuss the slow pace of institutional change in global gymnastics.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"decade","lemma":"decade","pos":"noun","he":"עשור","name":false},{"text":"elapsed","lemma":"elapse","pos":"verb","he":"חלף","name":false},{"text":"Indianapolis Star","lemma":"Indianapolis Star","pos":"proper noun","he":"אינדיאנפוליס סטאר","name":true},{"text":"published","lemma":"publish","pos":"verb","he":"פרסם","name":false},{"text":"initial","lemma":"initial","pos":"adjective","he":"ראשוני","name":false},{"text":"allegations","lemma":"allegation","pos":"noun","he":"האשמות","name":false},{"text":"sexual abuse","lemma":"sexual abuse","pos":"noun","he":"הת(א)טרדות מינית","name":false},{"text":"Larry Nassar","lemma":"Larry Nassar","pos":"proper noun","he":"לארי נאסאר","name":true},{"text":"former","lemma":"former","pos":"adjective","he":"לשעבר","name":false},{"text":"team doctor","lemma":"team doctor","pos":"noun","he":"רופא נבחרת","name":false},{"text":"USA Gymnastics","lemma":"USA Gymnastics"